HEARD l00 The first man to finish this Olympic marathon.
HEARD l01 got there by car.
HEARD l02 St. Louis, 1904, 90 degrees on dusty dirt roads.
HEARD l03 The man in charge set up just one water stop on purpose.
HEARD l04 He wanted to study dehydration.
HEARD l05 Officials drove cars ahead of the runners, so everyone ran in a cloud of dust.
HEARD l06 At mile nine, a runner named Fred Lors got cramps.
HEARD l07 So he hopped into a car and waved at the runners as he drove past them.
HEARD l08 10 miles later, the car broke down. So Fred jogged the rest.
HEARD l09 Fred crossed the line first. The crowd went wild.
HEARD l10 The president's daughter put a wreath on his head and almost handed him the gold.
HEARD l11 Then someone shouted, he came by car.
HEARD l12 The real leader, Thomas Hicks, wanted to lie down. His trainer said no.
HEARD l13 They gave him rat poison, mixed with egg whites, and a shot of brandy.
HEARD l14 then another dose.
HEARD l15 He started seeing things, his team carried him over the line, with his feet still running in the air.
HEARD l16 fourth place went to a Cuban mailman who had lost all his money gambling.
HEARD l17 He ran in his street clothes, cut into shorts.
HEARD l18 He snatched two peaches, eight rotten apples from an orchard.
HEARD l19 and took a nap and still came forth.
HEARD l20 Another runner got chased a mile off course by a dog.
HEARD l21 and Fred Lors, he was banned for life. The ban lasted six months.
HEARD l22 The next spring, he won the Boston Marathon.
HEARD l23 on foot this time.
